**Read:** [Fundamentals of Materials Science — MIT 3.012](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z), lecture 16 and interstitial/ionic examples; [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../../../../gaia/earth/mineralogy-and-petrology/sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), coordination and structural families; [Materials structure and observation — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF), Crystallography and Solid Solutions.

Coordination describes neighbours under a declared definition. A geometric site can accommodate a different species, an occupancy fraction or a vacancy, subject to chemical and structural constraints. Count sites, species and charge separately before treating a formula as a complete structure.

The radius-ratio construction estimates whether ideal touching spheres can occupy an interstice. It does not replace energetic, electronic or experimental evidence. Charge neutrality may couple substitutions or defects: replacing one cation can require a second substitution, a vacancy or a changed oxidation state.

**Work:** Count octahedral and tetrahedral sites in an identified close-packed cell. Construct a simple substitution account that preserves site balance and charge, then compare it with a measured or published composition. State which occupancies are measured, constrained or inferred. Explain why a satisfactory bulk formula may still permit several local arrangements.

**Return:** [Materials engineering](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
